eId IdType="pubmed">15953928</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Perri&#xe8;re G, Gouy M. WWW-query: an on-line retrieval system for biological sequence banks. Biochemie. 1996;78:364&#x2013;369.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">8905155</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Rapp&#xe9; MS, Giovannoni SJ. The uncultured microbial majority. Ann Rev Microbiol. 2003;57:369&#x2013;394.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">14527284</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Rogers SW, Moorman TB, Ong SK. Fluorescent in situ hybridization and microautoradiography applied to ecophysiology in soil. Soil Sci Soc Amer J. 2007;71:620&#x2013;631.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Roslev P, King GM. Survival and recovery of methanotrophic bacteria starved under oxic and anoxic conditions. Appl Environ Microbiol. 1994;60:2602&#x2013;2608.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC201690</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">16349336</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Shrestha M, Abraham W.-R, Shrestha PM, Noll M, Conrad R. Activity and composition of methanotrophic bacterial communities in planted rice soil studied by flux measurements, analysis of pmoA gene and stable isotope probing of phospholipids fatty acids. Environ Microbiol. 2008;10:400&#x2013;412.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">18177369</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Takeda K. Characteristics of a nitrogen-fixing methanotroph, Methylocystis T-1. Antonie van Leeuwenboek. 1988;54:521&#x2013;534.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">3148292</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Takeda K, Suzuki S, Neko K, Tomiyama Y, Fujita T, Matsuyama N, Watanabe I. Enumeration and characteristics of methanotrophs in paddy soils and rice roots. Jpn J Soil Sci Plant Nutr. 1998;69:570&#x2013;575. (in Japanese with English summary)</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Takeda K, Tonouchi A, Takada M, Suko T, Suzuki S, Kimura Y, Matsuyama N, Fujita T. Characterization of cultivable methanotrophs from paddy soils and rice roots. Soil Sci Plant Nutr. 2008;54:876&#x2013;885.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Vela GR, Wyss O. Improved stain for visualization of Azotobacter encystment. J Bacteriol. 1964;87:476&#x2013;477.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC277034</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">14151075</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Wagner M, Nielsen PH, Loy A, Nielsen JL, Daims H. Linking microbial community structure and functions: fluorescence in situ hybridization-microautoradiography and isotope arrays. Curr Opin Biotechnol. 2006;17:83&#x2013;91.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">16377170</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Wassmann R, Papen H, Rennenberg H. Methane emission from rice paddies and possible mitigation strategies. Chemosphere. 1993;26:201&#x2013;217.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Watanabe T, Kimura M, Asakawa S. Community structure of methanogenic archaea in paddy field soil under double cropping (rice-wheat) Soil Biol Biochem. 2006;38:1264&#x2013;1274.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Weisburg WG, Barns SM, Pelletier DA, Lane DJ. 16S ribosomal DNA amplification for phylogenetic study. J Bacteriol. 1991;173:697&#x2013;703.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC207061</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">1987160</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Whittenbury R, Phillips KC, Wilkinson JF. Enrichment, isolation and some properties of methane-utilizing bacteria. J Gen Microbiol. 1970;61:205&#x2013;218.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">5476891</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Wise MG, McArthur JV, Shimkets LJ. Methanotroph diversity in landfill soil: isolation of novel type I and type II methanotrophs whose presence was suggested by culture-independent 16S rDNA analysis. Appl Environ Microbiol. 1999;65:4887&#x2013;4897.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC91658</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">10543800</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Manual"><PMID Version="1">22441572</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2012</Year><Month>12</Month><Day>03</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2021</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>21</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print-Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1432-184X</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>64</Volume><Issue>2</Issue><PubDate><Year>2012</Year><Month>Aug</Month></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Microbial ecology</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Microb Ecol</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Effects of elevated CO2 on communities of denitrifying bacteria and methanogens in a temperate marsh microcosm.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>485</StartPage><EndPage>498</EndPage><MedlinePgn>485-98</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s00248-012-0036-8</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>The effects of elevated CO(2) on soil bacterial community with upland vegetation have been widely studied, but limited information is available regarding responses of denitrifier and methanogen communities to elevated CO(2) in wetland ecosystems. Using restriction fragment length polymorphism (RFLP), terminal RFLP analysis, and real-time quantitative PCR, we compared communities of denitrifiers and methanogens in a laboratory-scale wetland system planted with one of three macrophytes, Typha latifolia, Scirpus lacustris, or Juncus effusus, after 110 days of incubation. Our study showed that elevated CO(2) could affect community structures of both denitrifiers and methanogens, each of which had a unique response pattern. In particular, elevated CO(2) shifted nirS-containing community with a unique structure irrespective of vegetation type. mcrA-containing community appeared to shift to community with unique types of hydrogenotrophs under elevated CO(2) conditions. The change of dissolved organic carbon driven by elevated CO(2) appeared to be related with the shift of both denitrifiers and methanogens. Overall, this study indicates that elevated CO(2) could change the community structure of denitrifiers and methanogens temporarily. These results also suggest a presence of stable dominant populations that were not substantially affected by changes in CO(2) concentration.</AbstractText></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Lee</LastName><ForeName>Seung-Hoon</ForeName><Initials>SH</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>School of Civil and Environmental Engineering, Yonsei University, Seoul, 120-749, South Korea.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Kim</LastName><ForeName>Seon-Young</ForeName><Initials>SY</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Kang</LastName><ForeName>Hojeong</ForeName><Initials>H</Initials></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><DataBankList CompleteYN="Y"><DataBank><DataBankName>GENBANK</DataBankName><AccessionNumberList><AccessionNumber>DQ303099</AccessionNumber><AccessionNumber>DQ303100</AccessionNumber><AccessionNumber>DQ303101</AccessionNumber><AccessionNumber>DQ303102</AccessionNumber><AccessionNumber>DQ303103</AccessionNumber><AccessionNumber>DQ303104</AccessionNumber><AccessionNumber>DQ303105</AccessionNumber><AccessionNumber>DQ303106</AccessionNumber><AccessionNumber>DQ303107</AccessionNumber><AccessionNumber>DQ303108</AccessionNumber><AccessionNumber>DQ303109</AccessionNumber><AccessionNumber>DQ303110</AccessionNumber><AccessionNumber>DQ303111</AccessionNumber><AccessionNumber>DQ303112</AccessionNumber><AccessionNumber>DQ303113</AccessionNumber><AccessionNumber>D